# Yarn artifacts (Phase D.7d removed yarn from this monorepo; PnP-related
# E2E tests still touch .yarn/* when yarn is on PATH, so keep ignored).
.pnp.*
.yarn/

# Flatpak build artifacts — flatpak-builder build-dir + .flatpak-builder/
# cache + exported single-file bundles. Manifests + metainfo + flathub.json
# are kept (committed source); only build outputs ignored.
.flatpak-builder/
build-dir/
repo/
*.flatpak

# Native Vala/Rust bridge build artifacts. meson builds the cdylib into
# build/cargo-target (covered by build/ ignores); a direct `cargo` run in
# src/rust/ would otherwise leave a target/ dir here.
# `src/rust/Cargo.lock` is deliberately NOT ignored — every native bridge
# commits its lock so the crates.io half of the graph is pinned the way
# `check-refs-pin.mjs` pins the refs/ half. See each package's README.
packages/infra/*/src/rust/target/

# A BACKSTOP, not the fix. After ADR 0017 a bridge owns no `prebuilds/` — the
# artifacts live in the per-target `@gjsify/<x>-<os>-<arch>` packages — so a
# `prebuilds/` inside `packages/napi/napi/` is by definition not shippable: it is
# in no `files` list and no conformance rule can see it (they key on
# `gjsify.prebuilds`, which the bridge does not declare).
#
# It is listed because it REGREW: `napi.yml`'s macOS and Windows jobs staged
# straight into the bridge, six commits after #960 deliberately deleted the
# tracked copy for the linux sibling. Untracked AND unignored is the dangerous
# combination — one `git add -A` commits a binary back into the bridge. Those two
# jobs now stage where the stager resolves; this line is what stops the next hand
# from putting it back silently. The real mechanism is
# `tests/e2e/prebuild-change-gate`, which fails a workflow step that stages by
# hand at all.
packages/napi/napi/prebuilds/

# Vala-generated C + GIR handed from a valac host to a host that has no valac
# (today: the win32 webgl experiment in prebuilds.yml, via the package's
# `prebuilt_vala_c` meson option). Generation and consumption happen in the SAME
# workflow run, so these must NEVER be committed: a tracked copy would be a
# build input nothing regenerates and nothing byte-checks — the stale-artifact
# class AGENTS.md § Committed-artifact freshness exists to keep out. The ignore
# is the mechanism; the option's comment is only the explanation.
vala-c-export/

*.tsbuildinfo

node_modules/
tmp/
dist/
dist-templates/
lib/
build/
builddir/

# ADR 0002: `cli.gjs.mjs` and `tsc.gjs.mjs` are NO LONGER committed. They stay
# build outputs at these paths, npm-tarball entries and release assets; CI hands
# them to downstream jobs as the `bootstrap-bundles-fedora<v>` run artifact, and
# a fresh clone bootstraps from the PUBLISHED bundle (`gjs -m install.mjs`).
#
# The `dist/*` line below is NOT redundant and must not be tidied away. The
# global `dist/` rule above matches DIRECTORIES only, so the `!…/cli/dist/`
# re-include — which `affected.gjs.mjs` still needs — leaves every other child of
# that directory untracked AND unignored. Measured: before this line,
# `git check-ignore packages/infra/cli/dist/cli.gjs.mjs` matched nothing, so a
# single `git add -A` would have recommitted 6.6 MB. `tsc/dist` needs no such
# line because it re-ignores its whole directory again.
!packages/infra/cli/dist/
packages/infra/cli/dist/*
# Dedicated, Soup-free classifier bundle booted by the CI `changes` job on a
# plain ubuntu+gjs host (see .github/workflows/main.yml), BEFORE any install and
# gating every other job. It stays committed on purpose: its tables encode THIS
# commit's CI policy, so a published release's classifier would gate today's PR
# with last release's rules — stale, fail-open and invisible. Refreshed by
# `gjsify workspace @gjsify/cli build:affected-bundle`.
!packages/infra/cli/dist/affected.gjs.mjs
# @gjsify/tsc's default-library `lib/lib*.d.ts` are COMMITTED, exactly like the
# bundle above: version-locked runtime data tsc resolves on a consumer that has
# no upstream `typescript`. `package.json#files` ships them (`lib/lib*.d.ts`).
# NOTE: the v0.4.37–0.4.39 "empty lib/" regression (`error TS6053:
# lib.esnext.d.ts not found` + a `TS2318: Cannot find global type …` cascade on
# every consumer) was NOT a gitignore problem — the libs were always on disk at
# pack time. The real bug: gjsify's own packer (`cli/src/commands/pack.ts`) did
# NOT expand `files` GLOBS, so `lib/lib*.d.ts` matched nothing and shipped 0 files
# (a plain-dir entry like `["lib"]` worked; a glob did not). Fixed by implementing
# glob expansion in the packer. Committing the libs is an independent self-
# contained-tool choice (tracked-correct, like the bundle); the glob fix is what
# actually includes them. Rebuilt by `gjsify workspace @gjsify/tsc build`.
!packages/infra/tsc/lib/
!packages/infra/tsc/lib/*.d.ts

packages/**/*/test.*.js
packages/**/*/test.*.js.map
packages/**/*/test.*.cjs
packages/**/*/test.*.cjs.map
packages/**/*/test.*.mjs
packages/**/*/test.*.mjs.map
packages/**/*/conformance.*.js
packages/**/*/conformance.*.js.map

examples/**/*/test.*.js
examples/**/*/test.*.js.map
examples/**/*/test.*.cjs
examples/**/*/test.*.cjs.map
examples/**/*/test.*.mjs
examples/**/*/test.*.mjs.map

# Test artifacts (fs tests create temp dirs/files)
fs-*/
foobar/
symlink_tsconfig.json

# Ad-hoc test/debug scripts in project root — ANCHORED, which is what "in project root"
# always claimed. Unanchored, `test-*.mjs` matches at every depth, and it swallowed a new
# `scripts/` module that a gate imports (#1365): `git add` said nothing, and CI would have
# failed on the missing file only after the push. Nothing else in the tree matches.
/test-*.mjs
/test-*.js
.shoot-*.mjs

# Metafile
*.meta.json
meta.json

# Astro
.astro/

# E2E test artifacts
tests/e2e/*/out/

# Copied worker_threads fixtures (sources live under src/fixtures/; copied to
# the package root next to test.{gjs,node}.mjs by `prebuild:test:fixtures` so
# `new URL('./fixtures/...', import.meta.url)` resolves from the test bundle).
packages/node/worker_threads/fixtures/

# CI-only gjsify download cache (XDG_CACHE_HOME redirect target in
# .github/workflows/main.yml — never committed, restored by actions/cache).
.gjsify-cache/

# Claude Code agent worktree state (per-session, must never enter commits).
# `skills/` is the exception: those are SHARED, committed procedures that load on
# demand instead of sitting in AGENTS.md — a negation only works if the directory
# itself is not excluded, hence `.claude/*` rather than `.claude/`.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/

# Generated by `npm run status:generate` from status/ + the package manifests.
# NOT committed: its derived half counts directories on DISK, so an untracked
# scratch dir makes a developer tree and a clean CI checkout disagree by
# construction (measured: 68 vs 63 examples) — a committed copy could never be
# kept in agreement. The authored data under status/ is the tracked source.
/STATUS.md

# Playwright's HTML report, written by every browser test run and uploaded from
# `main.yml` as a CI artifact. One copy was committed by accident in #292 (a
# refactor PR that had nothing to do with it) and has been re-dirtying the tree
# on every local run since. Like the storybook theme copy, a file regenerated
# by the tooling has nothing to be checked against.
/tests/browser/playwright-report/
/tests/browser/test-results/

# `gjsify ship` output — the staged tree and the built .deb/.rpm. A build artifact,
# like dist/: reproducible from the bundle plus the manifest, and 6 MB per format.
# Unanchored on purpose: the output root is `gjsify.ship.outDir` and defaults to
# `ship` beside whatever project is being shipped, so it can appear at any depth.
ship/
# …which also matched the SOURCE directory implementing that command. Those files
# were already tracked, so nothing broke visibly — but `git add` on one of them is
# refused, and a NEW source file there would be silently unaddable, which is how it
# gets lost rather than reviewed.
!packages/infra/cli/src/utils/ship/
# …and the DOCS section for that command, which is untracked and therefore hit the
# silent case above: both pages were written, `git status` showed nothing, and a
# commit would have carried none of it.
!website/src/content/docs/ship/
# …and the e2e SUITE for that command — the THIRD instance of the same match, so it
# is listed here rather than discovered a fourth time, and the first one that could
# have broken a green tree: `run.mjs` predates the pattern and stayed tracked, so
# the suite kept working, while a NEW file beside it is silently unaddable. That is
# not hypothetical here — `fixture.mjs` is exactly such a file and both ship suites
# import it, so a `git add -A` would have landed two suites that die at
# ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND while every local run stayed green.
!tests/e2e/ship/

# Python bytecode. `.github/ship-oracle/*.py` are oracles a contributor imports
# by hand when reproducing a leg, and CPython writes `__pycache__/` beside them.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
